WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:03.860
Hey there crazies.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.300
I was just having this conversation with question
clone:

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.559
Why am I this size?

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.100
Well, you’re human and you're my clone, so you have to be this size.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.920
Then, why are humans this size?

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.680
Oh wow, that’s a great topic for a video! I've got to think about this.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.280
Hey! Where are you going?

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:19.960
So, let’s see what we can do with this.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.850
First, we could have just as easily been a
slightly different size

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.960
as we can see from the usual suspects.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.500
I mean, that’s just chance.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.140
Yeah, but why aren’t we way bigger?

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.940
Because not everything scales the same way

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.340
and a normal-sized human is kind of like a

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.440
scale model of a giant human,
so let’s make a list!

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:41.260
Things that scale with volume include:
mass, which can affect how you resist changes

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.880
in your motion,
Weight, which is how much gravity pulls down

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.680
on you,
and heat generated, because you have more

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.320
cells doing more stuff.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.740
Things that scale with area include:
bone &amp; muscle strength, because that’s related

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.860
to cross-section,
and heat dissipated, because that’s related

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.740
to exposed skin area.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.640
Side note!

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.780
Don’t ever let anyone tell you that you
lose more heat through your head.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:06.620
You lose heat through any exposed
skin, no matter where it is.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:07.580
End of side note!

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.420
OK, let’s say I made a clone that was, I
don’t know, 10 times taller,

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.980
They’d have 1000 times the mass, 1000 times
the weight,

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.800
and would be generating 1000 times as much
heat.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:18.720
Yikes!

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.080
But unfortunately, they’d only have 100
times the strength

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.040
and they’d only be dissipating 100 times
the heat.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.960
So, basically, they wouldn’t have the strength
to move their body,

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.240
their bones would break under the weight of
their body,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.180
and they’d burn up from the inside until
all their organs shut down.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:33.780
But?

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.580
Yes, there used to be things like dinosaurs
and mammoths

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:37.120
But?

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.180
Yes, there are still elephants and giraffes.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.140
But what about?

00:01:40.149 --> 00:01:43.419
And yes, floating can take some of the weight
off the bones and muscles

00:01:43.419 --> 00:01:46.479
so aquatic animals have an easier time being
bigger.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.080
The point is, humans can’t be that big.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.800
So, then why aren’t we way smaller?

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.220
Mmm, that one is a little harder.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.600
The big problem here is our thought holes.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.120
Also known as our brains.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.100
Brains!

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:03.900
Brain cells, or neurons, are some of the smallest cells in
the human body, at least in diameter,

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.340
and it still takes a brain this big to give
us our level of intelligence.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.680
But don’t elephants have?

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:10.850
OK, OK, it’s not just about brain size.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.800
Larger animals like elephants have bigger
brains than us.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.900
In fact, they have about 3 times as many neurons.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.519
But that doesn’t mean the elephant is 3
times smarter than us.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.980
Naturally bigger animals have bigger brains
because there’s more stuff to control.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:28.760
Over 97% of those elephant neurons are found
in the cerebellum.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.000
the part of the brain most responsible for
motor control.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.340
It needs all those neurons just to control
all its giant clunky parts.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.660
So, what about a brain-to-body mass ratio?

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.520
Something like that has been suggested before.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.600
We called it an “Encephalization Quotient”
or EQ,

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.640
which is the ratio between actual brain mass
and the brain mass we predict would be necessary

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:50.840
to control the body.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.020
Cats have an EQ of 1,

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:55.220
chimps about 2.5,

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.800
bottlenose dolphins between 4-5,

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.380
and humans sit comfortably at about 7.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.840
That certainly puts us on top!

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.020
But we usually attribute intelligence to the
cerebral cortex,

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:08.719
which is that wrinkly part on the outside
our brains

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.180
and only mammals have it.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.860
Which is pretty arrogant if you ask me, limiting intelligence
to mammals.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.080
Crows are pretty smart and they don’t have
a cerebral cortex.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:20.620
Octopuses are really smart and they don’t
have a cerebral cortex.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:24.060
In fact, 2/3 of their neurons are in their
arms,

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.560
which are not tentacles, by the way.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.720
These neurons allow each arm to act independently
of the others and the central brain,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.980
it’s a completely different kind of intelligence.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.480
OK, I concede, intelligence is complicated.

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:39.209
But there is another problem you’d run into
if you were smaller: your lungs.

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:43.400
If you get small enough, like insect small,
lungs are unnecessary.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.300
It would be more energy efficient to just
absorb oxygen through the skin.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.260
Insects do it through little holes in their
exoskeleton.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:53.629
Super Main Ultimate Point:
Different size organisms have to work with

00:03:53.629 --> 00:03:54.840
a different set of rules.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.460
Our rules only work at our size.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.680
But here’s the real question:
Why do we have any size at all?

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:03.940
We’ll try to find an answer on the next
episode of The Science Asylum.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:05.900
Make sure you subscribe so you don’t
miss it.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:07.680
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.080
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.340
In the last video, we talked Planck Units.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:15.580
Comment response time!

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:19.799
Rogue Assassin Gaming was wondering if Planck
Length is the shortest length,

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.440
then could Planck Time would be the shortest
amount of time?

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.540
Yes, in fact, the same models of quantum gravity
say that about length and time.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:28.690
But again, they don’t have any experimental
evidence.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:33.580
So, for now, Planck Time is just the time
it takes light to travel one Planck Length.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:38.320
and, as we said in the video, it was the age
of the universe when it was one Planck Length across.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.800
PapaKay heard one time that measuring the
Planck scale would create a black hole.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:43.979
Well, maybe.

00:04:43.979 --> 00:04:46.800
A typical way to measure the position of a
particle is to use light

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.280
and the wavelength of that light is your precision.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.330
To measure Planck Length, that photon has to have
an energy of 12 billion joules.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:55.410
or over 6 Planck Energies.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:59.100
Basically, you just turned a fruit fly into
one photon.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:04.000
That much mass-energy would make a black hole
if it were smooshed into a Planck Length.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.780
But, just because the photon has a wavelength
that small,

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.140
it doesn’t mean that’s the size of the
photon.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:12.680
Wavelength and size are not necessarily the
same thing.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:17.110
Camo Rovak suggested we should switch to a
Base-12 or Base-16 number system.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:19.840
You know, 12 does have a lot more factors
than 10,

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.400
so it would certainly make writing decimals easier.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.240
But I think you’d have a hard time convincing
anyone to change.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.870
We can’t even get the U.S. to switch to the metric system.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:32.440
Pablo had some very astute criticisms about
using black body radiation to define

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:33.660
Planck Temperature.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:38.080
For those of you who don’t know, black body
radiation is just light emitted by objects

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.700
due solely to it's temperature, so basically the vibrations and collisions between molecules.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:45.720
It’s commonly stated that if an object is
Planck Temperature hot,

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:49.020
then it would emit light that has a wavelength
of one Planck Length.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:53.440
But the problem with saying this is not
just one kind of photon is emitted by the object.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.479
Black body radiation is distributed over many
wavelengths.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:00.560
You’re emitting it right now, it’s just
mostly infrared.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:05.040
One Planck Temperature gives you a peak
wavelength of one Planck Length.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.800
Something could be at half a Planck Temperature
and still be emitting light at one Planck Length,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:10.880
just not as much.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:14.480
And to anyone like Infinity Pool who thinks
this channel doesn’t get enough attention.

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:16.300
Share the videos all the places!

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.220
